                        \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535033 false] [17592186327819 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Evaluate the improper integral:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=$$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535033 true] [17592186327819 1145 "For $R>4$, the partial-fraction identity gives\n\n$$\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx=\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}.$$\n\nBoth terms have the same finite upper bound. Integrating and combining the logarithms before taking a limit yields\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n&=\\left[\\ln(x-3)-\\ln(2x+1)\\right]_4^R\\\\\n&=\\ln\\frac{R-3}{2R+1}+\\ln9\n=\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}\n=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe limit is finite, so the improper integral converges. The two partial-fraction improper integrals each diverge when considered separately; keeping a common finite upper bound avoids an undefined subtraction." 13194139535033 true] [17592186327819 1145 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535033 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
